rs-matter 0.4.1: the last chunk of a long read can fail with Error::NoSpace and drop the exchange

ReportDataResponder::start_reply keeps 24 bytes of the reply buffer back for the closing
TLVs, but after the last attribute `report_attributes` closes the AttributeReports array
with `wb.end_container()?` and `report_events` opens the EventReports array with
`wb.start_array(..)?`, both inside that shortened buffer and without the chunk-and-retry
every other write has. When the last attribute leaves fewer than 3 free bytes (it depends
only on the exact size of the node's data) the write fails, the handler returns
Error::NoSpace, the exchange is dropped and the controller - matter.js sends a wildcard read
of attributes AND events right after commissioning - waits about 50 s before it reads again.
The same code is on rs-matter's `main` (checked 2026-10-03).

This lets those writes use the reserved space and keeps 16 bytes of it back again for the
closing TLVs of the chunk. Verified against fusion-firmware: the failing flow
(tests/matter-controller/attestation-check.sh: 8 of 8 runs hit NoSpace and took 84-87 s)
ran in 34-38 s with no NoSpace, and the strict sweep of 2400 attributes across 43 endpoints
(demo-check.sh) read cleanly. Apply with `patch -p1` inside the rs-matter-0.4.1 source.

